Experiment 1: Watching V8 in real time
This experiment proves that V8’s TurboFan optimizer watches your code. If you pass the exact same data type over and over (“Hot Code”), TurboFan optimizes it to pure machine code. If you suddenly change data types (“Monomorphic” vs. “Polymorphic” code), you confuse TurboFan, causing it to throw away its optimization (Deoptimization) and slow down.
1. Setup the Code
Create a file named v8-turbofan-test.js:
const { performance } = require('perf_hooks');
// A simple function that adds two values
function add(a, b) {
return a + b;
}
// 1. HOT CODE PATH (Monomorphic)
// We call the function 1,000,000 times using ONLY numbers.
// TurboFan will optimize this to lightning-fast machine code.
let start1 = performance.now();
for (let i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
add(1, 2);
}
console.log(`🚀 Monomorphic (Hot Code) execution time: ${performance.now() - start1}ms`);
// 2. CONFUSED CODE PATH (Polymorphic/Megamorphic)
// Now we break V8's assumptions by passing numbers, strings, and objects to the same function.
// TurboFan gets confused, bails out, and drops back to the slow interpreter.
let start2 = performance.now();
for (let i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
if (i % 3 === 0) add(1, 2);
else if (i % 3 === 1) add("hello", "world");
else add({ num: 1 }, { num: 2 });
}
console.log(`🐢 Polymorphic (Confused Code) execution time: ${performance.now() - start2}ms`);2. Run the Code
Execute the file in your terminal:
node v8-turbofan-test.js3. Analyze the Output

• The Hot Code path will run incredibly fast (often 1–3ms).

• The Confused Code path will take significantly longer (often 3x to 5x slower).

• Why? V8’s TurboFan compiled the first loop into raw assembly language optimized for CPU integers. The second loop forced V8 to dismantle that optimization on the fly because the data shapes kept changing.

Experiment 2: Watching V8’s Memory Heap & Garbage Collector
This experiment lets you watch V8 manage its Memory Heap. We will force V8 to allocate massive amounts of memory, watch the Garbage Collector fight to clean it up, and intentionally trigger a fatal “Out of Memory” crash.
1. Setup the Code
Create a file named v8-heap-test.js:
const v8 = require('v8');
function logHeapUsage() {
const heapStats = process.memoryUsage();
// Convert bytes to Megabytes for readability
const usedHeap = (heapStats.heapUsed / 1024 / 1024).toFixed(2);
const totalHeap = (heapStats.heapTotal / 1024 / 1024).toFixed(2);
console.log(`📊 V8 Memory Heap: ${usedHeap}MB used / ${totalHeap}MB allocated`);
}
console.log('🏁 Script started...');
logHeapUsage();
let kitchenStorage = [];
// Every 200ms, we dump a massive array into the Memory Heap
const cookInterval = setInterval(() => {
console.log('\n📦 Adding 10 million numbers to memory...'); // Allocate a massive block of memory
const heavyDish = new Array(10000000).fill(7);
kitchenStorage.push(heavyDish);
logHeapUsage();
// Stop after 5 rounds so the script doesn't crash your computer
if (kitchenStorage.length === 5) {
clearInterval(cookInterval);
console.log('\n🧹 Clearing kitchen storage reference...');
kitchenStorage = null; // Break the reference!
// Suggest to V8 to run Garbage Collection (if flag is enabled)
if (global.gc) {
global.gc();
console.log('✨ Garbage Collector ran manually!');
logHeapUsage();
} else {
console.log('💡 Run with --expose-gc to see the Garbage Collector instantly wipe this memory clean.');
}
}
}, 200);
2. Run the Code normally
node v8-heap-test.jsWhat you will see: The memory usage climbs rapidly. Once kitchenStorage = null happens, V8 realizes those arrays are no longer reachable from the root code, and it prepares to recycle that memory.
3. Run with Garbage Collection Controls
To see V8’s Garbage Collector instantly vaporize the unused memory on command, run Node with a special V8 engine flag:
node --expose-gc v8-heap-test.jsWhat you will see now: The moment global.gc() is called, the used heap memory drops right back down to almost zero!
4. Force a V8 Crash (Out of Memory)
What happens if you don’t clear the memory? By default, Node.js limits V8’s heap size so it doesn’t eat all your RAM. Let’s choke V8’s memory limits to force a crash. Run this command:
node --max-old-space-size=50 v8-heap-test.jsWhat you will see: V8 will allocate memory for the first loop or two, hit its artificial 50MB ceiling, panic because the Garbage Collector can’t free any memory (since kitchenStorage is still actively holding onto it), and crash with a terminal error: FATAL ERROR: Ineffective mark-compacts near heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory.
